#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478469} :activity/id #uuid "721b1290-aefc-5e37-b344-d5131a4a3506" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339 :topic/id #uuid "96c0d2ef-1891-5083-879c-8dcc09b70542" :topic/math-academy-id 1834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0589c295-d29a-5525-8df9-84a2fef5c818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360850} {:answer/id #uuid "07bef1ff-2469-5d35-b897-08a2224bc1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360863} {:answer/id #uuid "4e594d6b-dcde-5053-8f5b-521e676bfdea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361178} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7bf037-7a41-5080-8e72-6de80d6a14df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361463} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a6e881-f847-56c1-9261-cac4148a3edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360863} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d378b87-11c8-5248-9b4e-02b0168b1e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24945e7c-95e1-50bc-b79f-c2e7d2556ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-97593" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t),$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02094b1d-f4cb-5c2d-b251-9b08dea3ddda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58d19bde-c557-5f9c-8b97-9ee98b0b44b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,1⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263763} {:answer/id #uuid "8d133154-399b-5788-8d63-d8c5a7d9ea07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264207} {:answer/id #uuid "975c77d4-38ea-5a25-aa07-13a8a2dc63a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,0⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264300} {:answer/id #uuid "b5763ceb-8908-51fe-ab60-358749e774f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,-1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264569} {:answer/id #uuid "edaf1b77-f55a-53de-aaf6-870a334b4eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,0⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265077} :answer-field/id #uuid "61507bf1-0dd4-5fab-a0ea-a38287bd4d32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6665004f-e6b2-5e6c-b764-778c8eb359b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132201" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9eccffe8-ee2f-59ee-9cc6-9c221f7ead6a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e7b0c5-59c8-5276-8f27-e519196c22e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c697a54-9e06-52cc-9c8e-9e93cd4a2158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263796} {:answer/id #uuid "a532f5f7-12c2-5842-a3e6-eb768839607c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264413} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6b8756-6f83-5bd9-aa3b-ae5978576099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264473} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8a8631-251a-5a00-aa65-16be619ec141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264630} :answer-field/id #uuid "805bbad1-9820-5da9-8726-919fd6256468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d2943df-d322-57f9-9465-3be5dfbf7067" :question/math-academy-id "q-132129" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi }⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & ={e}^{-\\pi }\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =2{e}^{-\\pi }⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-\\pi }⟨0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times 2{e}^{-\\pi }⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{-\\pi }\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2{e}^{-2\\pi }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-3\\pi }}{2{e}^{-2\\pi }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1e1ea61-7fee-5b07-820f-6ba30cb6f254" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ea7b209-5616-55d5-9904-552d1c0715cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477851} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff08d81-960b-574d-bb5a-4735a65941c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477931} {:answer/id #uuid "623c10d1-ccda-5676-a53a-28fffcd4fe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477937} {:answer/id #uuid "6d62eea6-536d-5d3a-b711-a9c0a42702d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477961} {:answer/id #uuid "729b116e-a1cd-54d1-af16-82f64c0e08d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477851} :answer-field/id #uuid "3901cec7-c587-5a5c-8a5e-3e53db5169e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bee1b915-a838-5542-8a64-106e980bc53d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98666" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=1$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d82a98fc-1a8f-5783-a491-1003f56ccece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c45fb-1a1d-5177-bf4c-fd01be65faa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263096} {:answer/id #uuid "39c3dbdf-5f6f-5fe6-bac2-7cf9c7f67e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263514} {:answer/id #uuid "4350ceb2-104a-5954-9cd1-0a02d74f4a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263594} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccf270e-4167-5b66-a2b9-51b0179a8785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264341} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2ae9ed-c588-5454-885d-f17ba33f35b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263594} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef9e59a-6517-5986-94c0-f586c58a7db0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "258f49b8-8aac-592e-a98b-1492e2d635c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132122" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fca31df7-2371-581c-a00e-69db776a5a38" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478486}}] :activity/title "Radius of Curvature" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860454